diff-src — licensing
====================

These files come from OpenBSD's usr.bin/diff and do not share one license. The
full text of each is in the header of the file it applies to; this note is a
map, and a flag on the one term that carries an obligation beyond attribution.

| File                       | License                                                    |
| -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| `diff.c`, `diff.h`         | ISC (Todd C. Miller, 2003)                                 |
| `diffdir.c`                | ISC (Todd C. Miller, 2003)                                 |
| `xmalloc.c`, `xmalloc.h`   | ISC (Todd C. Miller / Damien Miller, from OpenSSH)         |
| `diffreg.c`                | **4-clause BSD** (Caldera International, 2001–2002) *and* 3-clause BSD (Regents of the University of California, 1991/1993) |

`diff_embed.h`, `diff_embed.c`, `diff_mod.c`, `diff.mdd` and this file are part
of zsh-wasm.

The clause worth knowing about
------------------------------

`diffreg.c` carries Caldera International's license for the original UNIX
sources, and its clause 3 is an advertising clause:

> 3. All advertising materials mentioning features or use of this software
>    must display the following acknowledgement:
>       This product includes software developed or owned by Caldera
>       International, Inc.

This is not the University of California's advertising clause, which Berkeley
rescinded in 1999; Caldera's is separate and still stands. It binds advertising
material, not the software or its documentation, and it is the same term under
which OpenBSD, FreeBSD and NetBSD have all shipped `diff` for two decades.

In practice: nothing about distributing this package triggers it. If zsh-wasm
is ever *advertised* on the strength of what it can do, that acknowledgement
belongs in the advertisement. A README feature list is not advertising material
under any reading anyone has taken of this clause, but the line is worth knowing
where it is.
